    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>This guide is general information, not legal advice. Camera and recording laws vary by state, county and city, and fair housing rules also apply. Check your local law, or ask a local attorney, before installing cameras at a rental.</p>

<h2>Who this guide is for</h2>
<p>Rental cameras raise different questions depending on which side of the lease you are on. Landlords want to protect the building, deter crime in shared areas and document damage, without invading tenants’ privacy or breaking the law. Tenants want to see who comes to the door and protect packages and cars, without losing their deposit to holes in the siding.</p>
<p>Both share one goal: a rental property outdoor camera with no subscription, meaning it works without a monthly fee. We cover the legal basics for each, then the hardware options that avoid a subscription, and finally a table of which features normally need a paid plan.</p>

<h2>Can a landlord have cameras outside a rental property?</h2>
<p>In many places, yes, with limits. The general principle across US states is that people have a reasonable expectation of privacy inside their homes and in spaces like bathrooms, but much less in exterior and shared areas that anyone could see. Landlords commonly place cameras on:</p>
<ul>
<li>Building entrances and exterior doors</li>
<li>Parking lots and driveways</li>
<li>Shared hallways, lobbies, mailrooms and laundry rooms (in multi-unit buildings)</li>
<li>Exterior of single-family rentals, such as the driveway and garage approach</li>
</ul>
<p>What landlords generally cannot do:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Put cameras inside a tenant’s unit</strong> while it is rented, including bedrooms, bathrooms and living areas.</li>
<li><strong>Aim exterior cameras into windows</strong> or into a tenant’s private yard or patio in a way that records private activity.</li>
<li><strong>Use cameras to harass or single out tenants</strong>, or in a way that could be seen as discriminatory. Fair housing rules apply; see <a href="https://www.hud.gov/program_offices/fair_housing_equal_opp" rel="noopener" target="_blank">HUD’s fair housing resources</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Hide cameras</strong> in places where tenants would reasonably expect privacy.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Disclosure in the lease</h3>
<p>Even where not strictly required, disclosing cameras is good practice. Add a clause to the lease listing where cameras are, what they record, whether audio is recorded, how long footage is kept and who can see it. Post visible signs at entrances. Clear disclosure prevents disputes and shows good faith if anything ever ends up in court.</p>
<h3>Audio is a different matter</h3>
<p>Recording conversations is covered by federal and state wiretap laws. Some states require only one party’s consent; others require all parties to consent. A camera that records audio in a shared hallway may capture conversations nobody agreed to. Many landlords simply turn audio recording off on rental cameras to stay clear of the issue. For an overview of how consent rules differ, see <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telephone_call_recording_laws" rel="noopener" target="_blank">call recording laws by jurisdiction</a> and confirm with your state’s statutes.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Short-term rentals</strong><p>If you rent through a short-term rental platform, it has its own camera disclosure rules and generally bans indoor cameras. Local ordinances may add more. Read the platform policy and your city rules.</p></div>

<h2>Can I have cameras on my rental property as a tenant?</h2>
<p>Usually, yes, but check your lease and ask your landlord. Tenants commonly add a doorbell camera or a small outdoor camera to watch their own entrance, car or package drop-off. The issues are usually about installation rather than recording:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Alterations clauses.</strong> Most leases forbid drilling, wiring or altering the exterior without permission.</li>
<li><strong>Shared spaces.</strong> In a multi-unit building, aiming a camera at a shared hallway or other tenants’ doors may break building rules or upset neighbors.</li>
<li><strong>HOA or building rules.</strong> Condos and managed buildings may have their own restrictions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Get permission in writing, even a short email. Explain what the camera points at and how it is mounted. Most landlords say yes to a <strong>tenant security camera</strong> that does not damage the building and only watches the tenant’s own door or parking spot.</p>
<figure><img src="/assets/img/tenant-camera.webp" alt="Battery camera on a no-drill clamp mount beside a rental apartment door" width="1200" height="800" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A battery camera on a clamp or adhesive mount leaves no holes and moves with you.</figcaption></figure>
<h3>No-drill installs for tenants</h3>
<p>Battery and solar cameras are ideal for renters because there is no wiring. Mounting options that leave no damage:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gutter mounts</strong> that clip to the edge of a gutter, often paired with a small solar panel.</li>
<li><strong>Clamp mounts</strong> on railings, fence posts, pergolas or balcony rails.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy-duty adhesive mounts</strong> on smooth surfaces such as siding or door frames (check the weight rating and test before trusting a camera to it).</li>
<li><strong>Doorbell cameras that use no-drill brackets</strong> on the door frame, battery powered.</li>
<li><strong>Indoor cameras looking out a window</strong>, with the caveat that infrared night vision reflects off glass.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our full guide to <a href="/how-to/install-without-drilling/">installing a camera without drilling</a> covers each option and its limits. When you move out, take the camera and mount with you, remove any adhesive residue, and factory reset the camera before reusing it; see <a href="/how-to/reset-solar-camera/">how to reset a solar camera</a>.</p>

<h2>Outdoor camera with no monthly fee: how local storage works</h2>
<p>An <strong>outdoor camera with no monthly fee</strong> stores its video somewhere you own, rather than on the manufacturer’s servers. There are three common approaches.</p>
<h3>microSD card in the camera</h3>
<p>The simplest option. Clips are saved to a card in the camera, typically 32–256 GB depending on the model. You view them through the app. Downsides: if someone steals the camera, the footage goes with it, and the card eventually fills up and overwrites old clips. The <a href="/tools/4g-data-and-storage-calculator/">data and storage calculator</a> estimates how many days a card holds.</p>
<h3>Local hub or base station</h3>
<p>Some systems pair cameras with a base station indoors (often called a HomeBase, hub or station depending on the brand) that holds storage. Footage is stored inside the building, so stealing the camera does not take the evidence. Hubs can also extend range and improve battery life on some systems.</p>
<h3>NVR (network video recorder)</h3>
<p>An NVR is a dedicated recorder with a hard drive, usually paired with wired PoE cameras or Wi-Fi cameras from the same brand. It can record 24/7 and keep weeks of footage. NVRs suit landlords with multi-unit buildings and power available at the camera locations; they are overkill for a tenant’s single door camera.</p>
<p>For landlords with exterior spots far from power, such as a parking lot or a detached garage, solar cameras with local storage avoid both the electrician and the subscription. Our <a href="/compare/solar-vs-wired/">solar vs wired comparison</a> breaks down the costs, and the <a href="/tools/solar-vs-wired-cost-calculator/">solar vs wired cost calculator</a> runs a five-year estimate.</p>

<h2>Which features need a subscription?</h2>
<p>Subscriptions are not a scam, but they are often unnecessary for rentals. What is included free and what is behind a paywall varies by brand, so always check the current plan details. As a general pattern:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Usually free</th><th>Often needs a plan</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Live view in the app</td><td>Yes</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motion alerts to your phone</td><td>Yes</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Local recording (microSD, hub, NVR)</td><td>Yes</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Basic motion detection and zones</td><td>Yes</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Person/vehicle detection</td><td>On many cameras (on-device)</td><td>Some brands (cloud-based)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Package, face or pet recognition</td><td>Some brands, on-device</td><td>Often</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cloud video history (30–60 days)</td><td>Rarely, or a few hours</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rich notifications with snapshot</td><td>Varies</td><td>Some brands</td></tr>
<tr><td>Professional monitoring</td><td>—</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cellular (4G) data</td><td>—</td><td>Yes, from a carrier</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Cloud storage does have one real advantage at a rental: footage is safe even if the camera is stolen or the internet is cut. If you skip cloud, choose a hub or NVR that keeps footage indoors, or accept the microSD risk.</p>

<h2>Best security cameras for rental property: what to look for</h2>
<p>The <strong>best security cameras for rental property</strong> depend on who owns them and where they go. A quick checklist:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Landlord</th><th>Tenant</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Power</td><td>Wired/PoE where possible; solar for parking and outbuildings</td><td>Battery or solar, no wiring</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storage</td><td>NVR or hub, no subscription</td><td>microSD or small hub</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mounting</td><td>Permanent, tamper-resistant, high</td><td>No-drill, removable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Audio</td><td>Usually off</td><td>Off, or two-way talk only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access</td><td>Owner/manager accounts with logs</td><td>Personal account, shared with roommates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Entrances, parking, common areas</td><td>Own door, own car, packages</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Battery and solar cameras suit both groups at exterior spots. Our <a href="/best-solar-security-cameras/">best solar security cameras</a> guide compares camera types, and <a href="/guides/how-many-cctv-cameras-do-i-need/">how many CCTV cameras do I need</a> helps with counts for a multi-unit building. The <a href="/tools/cctv-camera-planner/">CCTV camera planner</a> counts cameras zone by zone.</p>

<h2>Choosing a rental property outdoor camera with no subscription</h2>
<p>When you shop for a rental property outdoor camera with no subscription, read the product listing for three details before anything else: where video is stored, which detection features run on the camera itself, and what the free tier of the app includes. Marketing often says "no monthly fees" while quietly limiting free users to live view.</p>
<h3>Battery and solar cameras</h3>
<p>The default choice for tenants and for landlord spots without power. A small panel keeps the battery topped up, so nobody has to climb a ladder to recharge it every few months. Most store clips on microSD or a hub. Check the panel will get a few hours of direct sun at the mounting spot; the <a href="/tools/solar-camera-site-check/">solar camera site check</a> helps, and <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-need-direct-sunlight/">do solar cameras need direct sunlight</a> explains the limits of shaded spots.</p>
<h3>Wired and PoE cameras</h3>
<p>Best for landlords who own the building and want permanent, 24/7 recording at entrances and parking. A PoE camera carries power and data over one network cable to an NVR, so there is no battery to manage and no cloud required. Installation is more work and usually means drilling, which is fine for an owner and rarely fine for a tenant.</p>
<h3>Doorbell cameras</h3>
<p>A battery video doorbell is often the single most useful camera for a tenant, because the front door is where packages and visitors arrive. Check that the model records locally and that the free tier keeps recorded clips, not just live view.</p>
<p>Whichever type you choose, confirm the camera supports privacy masking, so you can black out a neighbor’s window or a shared area you should not be recording.</p>

<h2>Using footage to document damage and incidents</h2>
<p>Exterior footage can help landlords document vandalism, illegal dumping, unauthorized vehicles or a break-in, and help tenants document package theft or damage to their car. Keep it proportionate: cameras on common areas are for security and safety, not for tracking a tenant’s guests or schedule, which can quickly become a privacy or harassment issue.</p>
<p>If something happens, download and save the relevant clips immediately, because local storage overwrites old footage. Note the date, time and camera, and share copies with police or your insurer as needed. Our <a href="/guides/do-solar-cameras-work-at-night/">night performance guide</a> covers what to expect from footage after dark.</p>

<h2>Setting up rental property cameras the right way</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Check the law and the lease</strong><p>Look up your state’s rules on video surveillance and audio recording, and any city ordinances. Landlords: update the lease template. Tenants: read the alterations clause.</p></li>
<li><strong>Get permission or give notice</strong><p>Tenants ask in writing. Landlords notify existing tenants before adding cameras, in writing, and post signs.</p></li>
<li><strong>Plan the views</strong><p>Cover entrances, parking and shared areas. Avoid windows, private yards and neighbors’ doors. Use privacy masking in the app to black out anything you should not record.</p></li>
<li><strong>Choose local storage</strong><p>microSD for a single camera, hub or NVR for several. Set a retention period and stick to it.</p></li>
<li><strong>Turn audio off unless you are sure</strong><p>Two-way talk for answering the door is one thing; recording conversations is another.</p></li>
<li><strong>Secure the accounts</strong><p>Use strong passwords and two-factor authentication. When tenants move out, landlords should confirm former tenants no longer have access, and tenants should remove the camera from their account before handing it over.</p></li>
</ol>

